The Art Of...
=============
Live site: https://thebalancesheet.netlify.app (rename in Netlify's
site settings whenever you want a URL that matches the new name).
Deploy: push to GitHub main -> Netlify builds and deploys automatically.

FILES
-----
index.html            Landing page ("Enter Site")
home.html              Homepage
gallery.html           Gallery of artwork, with a click-to-enlarge view
journal.html           Full list of journal entries
about.html             About page
contact.html           Contact page + form
post.html              Renders one full journal entry (?post=... in the URL)
style.css              All styling (one shared stylesheet)
script.js              Loads content from Sheets, scroll animations, contact form
posts-template.csv     Starter template if you ever rebuild the Posts tab
images/                Site images (mostly unused now -- see note below)

THE BIG IDEA: ALMOST EVERYTHING LIVES IN ONE GOOGLE SHEET
-----------------------------------------------------------
"The Art Of... — Content" is one Google Sheet with three tabs:

  Posts          Your journal entries
  Artwork        Your gallery pieces
  Page Content   Every other piece of text and link on the site

  https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1HLti9dIu5B8Z7WJSiq5N1ArrE_LkkxGW_rakZIx3k0I/edit

script.js already has this sheet's ID wired in (SHEET_ID near the top
of the file) and sharing is set to "Anyone with the link -> Viewer" --
that's what makes it readable by the site but editable only by
whoever is signed into the Google account that owns it (you).
Visitors can only ever read it, same as any public page.

The only things NOT editable from the sheet are the site's name ("The
Art Of...") and the browser-tab titles -- those stay in the HTML on
purpose, so the brand never disappears by accident.

Changes usually show up within a minute of reloading the page -- no
rebuild or redeploy needed, since the site fetches the sheet fresh
every time someone visits.

TAB 1: POSTS (your journal)
------------------------------
  Title        The entry's title.
  Category     Anything you want (e.g. Process, Life, Inspiration). The
               site automatically gives each distinct category its own
               color, so you're not limited to fixed options.
  Date         e.g. 2026-09-29. Controls sort order (newest first).
  Summary      One or two sentences shown on the preview card.
  Image URL    Optional. Paste a direct image link, or leave blank for
               a plain color block instead.
  Published    Type "Yes" to make it go live, "No" to keep it a draft.
  Body         The full entry. Press Ctrl+Enter (Windows/Linux) or
               Cmd+Return (Mac) inside the cell to start a new
               paragraph -- each one becomes its own paragraph on the
               site.

Add an entry by adding a row. Edit one by editing its row. Take one
down by switching Published to "No" or deleting the row.

TAB 2: ARTWORK (your gallery)
--------------------------------
  Title          The piece's title.
  Medium         e.g. Graphite, Oil, Watercolor, Digital.
  Date           Controls sort order (newest first).
  Image URL      Required -- a piece with no image never shows up.
  Description    A sentence or two, shown when someone clicks to
                 enlarge the piece.
  Published      "Yes" to show it, "No" to keep it a draft.

Getting an "Image URL": a Google Drive share link does NOT work
directly (it opens a Drive page, not the raw image). The easiest free
option is imgur.com -- drag your photo in, no account needed, then
copy the "Direct Link" it gives you (it ends in .jpg/.png/etc). Paste
that into the Image URL column.

TAB 3: PAGE CONTENT (everything else)
----------------------------------------
Two columns: Key and Value. Each row's Key matches one specific piece
of text or link on the site -- change the Value and that's what shows
up. Nothing else about the row matters (order doesn't matter, and nav
labels, headings, body text, and social links are all mixed together
in whatever order you like).

A few notable keys:
  Site / Nav Home, Site / Nav Gallery, etc.   The 5 nav labels
  Home / Hero Title, Home / Hero Body, etc.   The homepage's main text
  About / Name, About / Bio Paragraph 1, etc. Your bio
  About / Photo URL                           Leave blank to keep the
                                               "ND" monogram, or paste
                                               an image link to swap in
                                               a real photo
  Contact / Email                             Also updates the
                                               "Email Directly" link
                                               automatically
  Contact / Instagram URL, Twitter URL, etc.  Where those buttons go

If you ever delete a row by accident, the site just falls back to
whatever text is already written into the HTML for that spot -- it
never breaks or shows blank.

IF YOU EVER NEED TO RECONNECT THE SHEET
------------------------------------------
1. Copy the Sheet's ID from its URL:
     https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/THIS_LONG_ID_HERE/edit
2. Open script.js, find SHEET_ID near the top, paste the ID in, commit
   and push.
3. Make sure the new sheet has three tabs named exactly "Posts",
   "Artwork", and "Page Content" (tab names are case-sensitive), and
   that sharing is "Anyone with the link -> Viewer".

CONTACT FORM (no backend code needed)
----------------------------------------
The contact form uses Netlify Forms, a feature built into Netlify
itself, so there's no server, database, or API key to manage:

1. Netlify scans contact.html when it deploys and notices the
   <form data-netlify="true"> tag.
2. Whenever someone submits the form, Netlify saves it for you.
3. View submissions any time at: Netlify dashboard -> your site ->
   "Forms".
4. To get an email every time someone submits: Netlify dashboard ->
   Forms -> Settings -> "Add notification" -> "Email notification".

Spam protection: there's a hidden "bot-field" input (the honeypot).
Real visitors never see or fill it in, but spam bots usually fill in
every field they find -- so if it's ever non-empty, Netlify quietly
throws the submission away. If spam ever becomes a real problem, you
can turn on Netlify's optional reCAPTCHA from the same Forms settings
page -- no code changes required.

Note: the form only works once it's actually deployed on Netlify --
opening contact.html directly from your computer (or a plain local
preview) won't submit anywhere, that's expected.
